                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2838780,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "So how did maple syrup production begin? Well, long before Europeans settled in these parts of the world, the indigenous communities had started producing maple sugar. They bored holes in the trunks of maple trees and used containers made of tree bark to collect the liquid sap as it poured out. As they were unable to keep the liquid for any length of time, they didn't have storage facilities in those days, they boiled the liquid by placing pieces of rock that had become scorching hot from the sun into the sap. They did this until it turned into sugar, and they were then able to use this to sweeten their food and drinks.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2838781,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "So how did maple syrup production begin?",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 196.89502,
                  "from": 193.455,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
