                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2838900,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "Since that time, improvements have been made to the process, but it has changed very little overall. So let's look at the production of maple syrup today. Clearly, the maple forests are a valuable resource in many Canadian and North American communities. The trees have to be well looked after, and they cannot be used to make syrup until the trunks reach a diameter of around 25 centimeters. This can take anything up to forty years.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2838901,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Since that time, improvements have been made to the process, but it has changed very little overall.",
