                  "from": 277.445,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2838981,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "As I've already mentioned, maple trees need the right conditions to grow and also to produce sap. Why is this? Well, what happens is that during a cold night, the tree absorbs water from the soil and that rises through the tree's vascular system. But then in the warmer daytime, the change in temperature causes the water to be pushed back down to the bottom of the tree. This continual movement up and down leads to the formation of the sap needed for maple syrup production.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2838982,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "As I've already mentioned, maple trees need the right conditions to grow and also to produce sap.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 289.01,
                  "from": 281.36502,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2839000,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Why is this?",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 290.53,
                  "from": 289.41,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2839004,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Well, what happens is that during a cold night, the tree absorbs water from the soil and that rises through the tree's vascular system.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 301.09,
                  "from": 290.84998,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2839029,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "But then in the warmer daytime, the change in temperature causes the water to be pushed back down to the bottom of the tree.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 310.005,
                  "from": 301.765,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2839054,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "This continual movement up and down leads to the formation of the sap needed for maple syrup production.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 319.13,
                  "from": 310.565,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2839073,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "When the tree is ready, it can be tapped and this involves drilling a small hole into the trunk and inserting a tube into it that ends in a bucket. The trees can often take several taps, though the workers take care not to cause any damage to the healthy growth of the tree itself. The sap that comes out of the trees consists of 98% water and 2% sugar and other nutrients. It has to be boiled so that much of that water evaporates, And this process has to take place immediately using what are called evaporators. These are basically extremely large pans.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2839074,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "When the tree is ready, it can be tapped and this involves drilling a small hole into the trunk and inserting a tube into it that ends in a bucket.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 331.695,
                  "from": 320.65,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2839105,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The trees can often take several taps, though the workers take care not to cause any damage to the healthy growth of the tree itself.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 341.69498,
                  "from": 332.335,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2839131,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The sap that comes out of the trees consists of 98% water and 2% sugar and other nutrients.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 351.29,
                  "from": 343.05,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2839150,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "It has to be boiled so that much of that water evaporates, And this process has to take place immediately using what are called evaporators.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 362.265,
                  "from": 351.77,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2839176,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "These are basically extremely large pans.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 366.985,
                  "from": 363.145,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2839183,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "The sap is poured into these. A fire is built and the pans are then heated until the sap boils. As it does this, the water evaporates and the syrup begins to form. The evaporation process creates large quantities of steam and the sap becomes thicker and denser and, at just the right moment when the sap is thick enough to be called maple syrup, the worker removes it from the heat. After this process, something called sugar sand has to be filtered out as this builds up during the boiling and gives the syrup a cloudy appearance and a slightly gritty taste.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2839184,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The sap is poured into these.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 369.065,
                  "from": 367.305,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2839191,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "A fire is built and the pans are then heated until the sap boils.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 374.87003,
                  "from": 369.59,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2839206,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "As it does this, the water evaporates and the syrup begins to form.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 380.55002,
                  "from": 375.75,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2839220,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The evaporation process creates large quantities of steam and the sap becomes thicker and denser and, at just the right moment when the sap is thick enough to be called maple syrup, the worker removes it from the heat.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 397.235,
                  "from": 381.79498,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2839260,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "After this process, something called sugar sand has to be filtered out as this builds up during the boiling and gives the syrup a cloudy appearance and a slightly gritty taste.",
